# frozen_string_literal: true

# Generated by shipshape. This file is yours now.
#
# **Where personal data is, declared once, in code.**
#
# The right to erasure is unimplementable without this. Not difficult — unimplementable: you
# cannot delete what nobody can enumerate, and in a schema of two hundred tables nobody can
# enumerate it from memory. Every erasure request then becomes a fresh archaeology, answered
# by whoever is on duty, and the answer differs each time.
#
# **This file is the fact, not a description of one.** A column is personal because it is
# listed here; nothing infers it. That is what makes it survive — a guess about column names
# would go stale the day somebody adds `contact_ref`, silently, which is the failure mode
# every checked-in list in this repository is built to avoid.
#
# **It cannot go stale, because a column that is not classified fails the build.**
# `Shipshape/PersonalDataIsDeclared` reads the schema and asks this file about every column
# whose name suggests a person. A new one is an offence until somebody writes a line here —
# and writing that line is the decision, made once, by whoever added the column and still
# remembers what it holds.
#
# **What this is not.** It is not a compliance record and nothing here says you are lawful.
# It says where the data is and what happens to it on erasure. Whether you had a basis to
# collect it, whether the retention is justified, whether an anonymisation is irreversible in
# fact — those are judgements, and no file and no cop makes them.
module PersonalData
  # What happens to a column when a person asks to be forgotten.
  #
  # - `:delete_row`          the whole row goes; the column needs no separate handling
  # - `:anonymise`           the row stays, the column is overwritten with a non-identifying
  #                          value — a counter, a hash with the key destroyed, a constant
  # - `:retain_with_reason`  it stays, and the reason is written next to it. A legal hold, a
  #                          statutory retention period, a financial record. This is a real
  #                          answer and it is the one that must never be silent.
  # - `:not_personal`        it matched the pattern and is not about a person —
  #                          `filename`, `class_name`, `host_address`. Say so once and the
  #                          guard stops asking.
  ROUTES = %i[delete_row anonymise retain_with_reason not_personal].freeze

  # table => { column => route }
  #
  # Written flat and literal on purpose: it is read by a cop with a regular expression, and
  # anything computed here would be invisible to it. Add the reason as a comment where the
  # route is `:retain_with_reason` — that comment is the only record of why.
  COLUMNS = {
    # "users" => {
    #   "email"       => :anonymise,
    #   "name"        => :anonymise,
    #   "ip_address"  => :delete_row,
    #   "class_name"  => :not_personal,
    # },
    # "invoices" => {
    #   # Statutory: financial records are kept seven years (Companies Act s.388).
    #   "billing_name" => :retain_with_reason,
    # },
  }.freeze

  def self.route(table, column)
    COLUMNS.fetch(table, {})[column]
  end

  def self.declared?(table, column)
    !route(table, column).nil?
  end

  # Every table holding something that has to be erased or anonymised — the work list an
  # erasure command is written against, and the thing nobody can hold in their head.
  def self.tables_needing_erasure
    COLUMNS.select { |_, columns| columns.value?(:delete_row) || columns.value?(:anonymise) }
           .keys.sort
  end
end
